|~ `::point`, the kind of a place in source text, and what is declared
   under it: the words that read its offset, its line and its column.
   The decisions it exists for: [D249]. ~|

:point
  |~~ A point is a place in source text, where a span starts or ends:
      three whole numbers under its tag, the offset from zero, the line
      and the column from one, `::point[0 1 1]` before the first
      character of a text. `offset`, `line` and `column` read them.
      ::link~(::span | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ The three numbers, the offset, the line and the column. ~~| [::number]
    | holds :PayloadNotThreeNumbersError
        |~~ A point holds three whole numbers, its offset, its line and
            its column.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf ~(count | eq 3) ~(every ~(eq (floor))))
    | proves :PayloadNotThreeNumbersError/refusesSixNumbers
        |~~ Six numbers make a span, no point. ~~|
        ~(::point[0 1 1 4 1 5] !| type | eq ::point/PayloadNotThreeNumbersError)
    | proves :PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no vector of numbers. ~~|
        ~(::point{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} !| type | eq ::point/PayloadNotVecError)

:point/offset
  |~~ Answers the offset of the point from the start of the text,
      counted from zero, `::point[4 1 5] | offset` answering `4`.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The offset, from zero. ~~| ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotPointError
        |~~ `offset` reads a point, the start or the end of a span,
            `"[1" | parse !| get /location | start | offset`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsTheFirstNumber
        |~~ The offset is the first number of a point. ~~|
        ~(::point[4 1 5] | offset | eq 4)
    | impl ~(get /0)

:point/line
  |~~ Answers the line of the point, counted from one,
      `::point[4 2 1] | line` answering `2`.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The line, from one. ~~| ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotPointError
        |~~ `line` reads a point, the start or the end of a span,
            `"[1" | parse !| get /location | start | line`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsTheSecondNumber
        |~~ The line is the second number of a point. ~~|
        ~(::point[4 2 1] | line | eq 2)
    | impl ~(get /1)

:point/column
  |~~ Answers the column of the point in its line, counted from one,
      `::point[4 1 5] | column` answering `5`.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The column, from one. ~~| ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotPointError
        |~~ `column` reads a point, the start or the end of a span,
            `"[1" | parse !| get /location | start | column`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsTheThirdNumber
        |~~ The column is the third number of a point. ~~|
        ~(::point[4 1 5] | column | eq 5)
    | impl ~(get /2)

